 [17592186566969 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566969 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c69bcd40-ed79-55be-8b7b-cb7c0491af3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567011 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "059319c8-1dff-5dfe-8695-5074282cdfe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567011 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-{z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567021 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(7y-6x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1691ed28-0c5c-54a8-9012-9dfc00861f65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567021 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567042 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d644ebc-b214-5b17-9daa-3ced5a3257b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567042 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567047 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67e10996-e62d-5489-9a64-73c5e56228d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567047 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567052 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567052 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567052 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567052 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(3,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "738ade6c-0972-56b4-b1bb-9f8fdeb237e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567052 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (0+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3+1}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567072 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ for $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6bdefd5-aef3-5d16-afc3-d28fde40b19b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567072 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-x\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{4})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot x\\cdot 4{z}^{3} \\\\  & =-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567081 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7285b21-99ee-5b47-b1b3-ef79da9b3c2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567081 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =(3{x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567093 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567093 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567093 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567093 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567093 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfe77a47-89cf-58c9-a7e4-db0e7d6a133d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567093 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differenciation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+{y}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
